WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.870
Cannibalism, asexuality, and violence.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.970
A society that had collapsed.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:12.760
What’s going on here?

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:22.690
In 1972, John B Calhoun detailed the specifications
of an utopia designed for mice: built in the

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:23.899
laboratory.

00:00:23.899 --> 00:00:29.330
Every aspect of Universe 25, as this particular
model was called, was designed to cater for

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:35.980
the well-being of its rodent residents, increase
their lifespan, and allow them to mate.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:41.150
There was abundant food, water, and nesting
material.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.960
The universe was cleaned regularly.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.770
There were no predators, the temperature stable.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:47.950
Paradise.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:51.940
Or maybe not?

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.570
Four pairs of disease-free mice, selected
from the National Institutes of Health’s

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:01.760
elite breeding colony, moved in on day one.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.490
It took months for the rodents to familiarize
themselves with their new world.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:13.060
Then they started to reproduce and the population
increased exponentially, doubling every fifty-five

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:14.060
days.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.080
Those were the good times in paradise.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:26.020
Past day 315, more than six hundred mice now
lived in Universe 25, rubbing shoulders on

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.450
their way up and down the stairwells to eat,
drink, and sleep.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.829
Population growth slowed.

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:40.050
Young ones found themselves born into a world
with far more mice than meaningful social

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.250
roles.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.439
Males faced a lot of competitors to defend
their territory against.

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:48.969
Many found that so stressful, they gave up.

00:01:48.969 --> 00:01:54.320
Normal discourse within the community broke
down, and with it the ability of mice to form

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.770
social bonds.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:02.549
Lone females retreated to isolated nesting
boxes on penthouse levels.

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:09.300
Other males, a group Calhoun termed “the
beautiful ones,” never sought sex and never

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:16.090
fought — they just ate, slept, and groomed,
wrapped in narcissistic introspection.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:22.400
Elsewhere, cannibalism, asexuality, and violence
became endemic.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.440
Mouse society had collapsed.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.890
On day 560 the population peaked at 2,200
mice.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:36.440
A few survived past weaning until day six
hundred, after which there were few pregnancies

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.810
and no surviving young.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:46.300
As the population had stopped regenerating
itself, its path to extinction was clear.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:51.700
The mice had lost the capacity to rebuild
their numbers—many that could still conceive,

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.420
such as the “beautiful ones” and their
secluded singleton female counterparts, had

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:00.090
lost the social ability to do so.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:03.700
On day 920 was the last conception.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:12.920
The last mouse died on May 23 1973, four years
and ten months after colonization.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.620
Calhoun later said that the creatures had
died two deaths.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.010
The first was that of their spirit and their
society.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.950
The “second death” was that of their physical
body.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:28.190
It was not the first time the ethologist had
built a world for rodents.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:34.980
Colhoun had been creating utopian environments
for rats and mice since the 1940s, with consistent

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:41.430
results: overpopulation leads to explosive
violence and hypersexual activity, followed

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.810
by asexuality, self-destruction, and extinction.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:53.230
In his widely cited paper, “Population Density
and Social Pathology”, Calhoun concluded:

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:58.930
No matter how sophisticated we are, once the
number of individuals capable of filling social

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:05.450
roles greatly exceeds the number of such roles,
only violence and disruption can follow.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:10.260
He then referred to a phenomenon he called
“Behavioral Sink”

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.480
Behavioral sink is our desire to be in the
presence of others, to be conditioned to seek

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.359
to be near others, and to be drawn to the
crowd, in spite of the conflicts that this

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:22.470
can generate.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:27.650
Drawing from Calhoun's popular research, social
scientists started to call for restrictions

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:33.410
on reproduction as the only possible response
to the world's rising population.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:36.250
Calhoun himself was more optimistic about
our future.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:42.630
He argued: as our physical space declines,
we are forced to extend a conceptual space

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:47.699
— our network of ideas and technologies.

00:04:47.699 --> 00:04:52.610
Later in his career he turned to possible
solutions and began to build creative universes

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:55.470
that minimize the ill effects of overcrowding.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:58.009
What are your thoughts?

00:04:58.009 --> 00:05:02.699
Is overcrowding a danger for mankind or does
it only affect rodents?

00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:06.110
And if so, what can save the human psyche?

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:11.449
Avoiding eye contact in crowded places is
one strategy, but is that enough?

00:05:11.449 --> 00:05:18.340
To read more about Universe 25, and its cultural
impact, read the paper of Edmund Ramsden &amp; Jon

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:19.340
Adams.

